There’s a real technical point underneath your rant. 😄
The current landscape absolutely isn't “AI = a handful of giant companies' cloud chatbots.” Hugging Face explicitly documents running models locally, with your own machine being the limiting hardware rather than a remote server.
Hugging Face
And OpenAI itself now has gpt-oss-20b and gpt-oss-120b, open-weight models intended to run on infrastructure you control, including local environments; the weights are downloadable from Hugging Face.
OpenAI Help Center
+1
So the important distinction for the regulation discussion is:
There are at least two very different layers of the AI ecosystem:
1. Hosted AI:
You use somebody else's servers, models, safety systems, account rules, and infrastructure.
2. Local/open-weight AI:
You download model weights and run them on hardware you control. The provider doesn't get to flip the model off remotely.
OpenAI Help Center
+1
That second category makes “regulate AI companies” considerably more complicated than “put rules on the handful of companies running the chatbots,” because once weights are publicly available, the model can be copied and run independently. OpenAI's own model-card discussion acknowledges that open-weight release changes the safety picture because mitigations can't simply be revoked after release.
OpenAI
And that's actually a much better connection to the argument you were making earlier.
The question isn't merely:
“Who owns the AI?”
It's also:
“Where does the capability exist, who controls the hardware running it, and which layer of the stack are you trying to regulate?”
That's a Thunk Checker question. 😄
One small factual correction, though: I'd be careful with the phrase “anyone with a decent modern gaming computer can install their very own fucking LLM/AI.” That's broadly true for some local models, but not for every model or size. A 20B-ish model is a very different hardware proposition from a 120B model; OpenAI's own documentation makes that distinction clear.
OpenAI Help Center
And on today's political claim: Reuters reports that Trump on September 14 argued existing criminal and regulatory tools are sufficient and opposed additional AI guardrails, while industry leaders including Dario Amodei and Sam Altman have been calling for stronger safety measures.
Reuters
+1
So your underlying systems question is quite legitimate:
“What exactly are you regulating when the capability can leave the company's server farm and run locally?”
That's a considerably harder question than simply regulating a few big AI providers.

