Yep — this is actually one place where your irritation has a factual basis, with one important qualification.
The U.S. government does not operate from one universal, context-free definition of “AI.” A current Congressional Research Service report explicitly says there is no official U.S. government definition of artificial intelligence and notes that policymakers use the term in different ways.
Congress.gov
At the same time, that doesn't mean federal policy is literally operating with no definitions. Different statutes and agencies adopt particular definitions for particular purposes. For example, 15 U.S.C. §9401 provides a statutory definition used by federal AI policy, and current White House memoranda explicitly incorporate that definition.
The White House
+1
And it gets wonderfully messy from there.
NIST's current glossary lists multiple definitions of AI from different source documents, including systems that learn from data, systems performing human-like cognitive tasks, techniques approximating cognitive tasks, and systems making predictions, recommendations, or decisions. NIST explicitly warns that those definitions are context-dependent rather than one universal preferred definition.
NIST Computer Security Resource Center
+1
So your Thunk Checker objection is exactly the right first question:
“When you say you want to regulate AI, WHAT EXACTLY ARE YOU DEFINING AS AI?”
Because “AI” could mean a trained model, an AI-enabled application, an automated decision system, a whole deployed system, or even portions of the infrastructure supporting it. Recent federal documents actually distinguish among AI models, AI systems, and automated decision systems for precisely that reason.
Congress.gov
+1
And that matters enormously to the point you raised earlier about local models.
You can't sensibly write one rule for “AI” and assume it maps neatly onto:
OpenAI/Anthropic/Google cloud service → downloadable model weights → Ollama/vLLM → local application → hardware → automated system.
