9-15-26 10:48pm  🔮 GPT
Oh. Now I really like the idea.
And the funny part is: people absolutely have built things very close to what you're describing. Your idea is not “a weird PC with no screen.” It's essentially an embedded inference appliance: a tiny Linux computer whose only job is to host the model and expose an interface to another computer. NVIDIA Jetson is explicitly designed for this sort of on-device AI, and Raspberry Pi now sells an AI HAT+ 2 specifically for local LLM/VLM workloads.
NVIDIA Perspectives
+1
But your architecture has a nice twist: the host PC doesn't need to run the model at all. It just supplies the model and talks to the little brain.
A) Has anyone done it?
Yes. Very close.
The cleanest off-the-shelf example is probably Jetson Orin Nano Super. It's a tiny Linux machine with a 6-core ARM CPU, 8 GB unified LPDDR5, 1,024 CUDA cores and 102 GB/s memory bandwidth. NVIDIA explicitly documents running LLMs on it, including models up to roughly 8B parameters, and supports stacks such as Ollama, llama.cpp, vLLM and TensorRT-LLM. Current NVIDIA pricing lists the Orin Nano Super Developer Kit at $399.
NVIDIA Developer
+1
There's an even more literal example of your design philosophy: Raspberry Pi 5 + AI HAT+ 2. The HAT has its own 8 GB memory and is specifically intended to run LLMs while leaving the Pi as the host/controller. Raspberry Pi says it can expose the local model through POST/API calls, including via its Hailo-Ollama server. The HAT itself is currently $200; a Pi 5 is separate.
Raspberry Pi
+1
So yes:
“USB, CPU, RAM, GPU/NPU, no monitor, no keyboard, host talks to it”
is absolutely a real class of machine.
B) How cheap could your version be?
Here's where I think your idea gets interesting.
You don't need to build a motherboard from scratch. That's the part that would make this crazy expensive.
Start with an existing compute board/module and make your appliance around it.
For example:
Cheap little brain
Raspberry Pi 5 8 GB: currently listed at $95.
Raspberry Pi
+1
Add AI HAT+ 2: $200.
Raspberry Pi
Then add power supply, case/cooling and storage.
You're roughly in the $350–$500-ish appliance neighborhood before depending on exactly what storage/case/power hardware you choose.
But there's a catch: Raspberry Pi/Hailo's current AI HAT+ 2 is aimed at LLMs up to around 6B parameters, so this is not the “run a giant 30B/70B model” box.
Raspberry Pi
The Jetson version is more capable, but you're more like $400+ before accessories, and its 8 GB memory is still your governing constraint. NVIDIA documents the Nano Super at around the 8B class.
NVIDIA Developer
C) Is it practical versus buying a new PC?
For you specifically? I think there's a real case for it.
Not because it is cheaper than every new PC.
Because it gives you a completely different machine:
Host PC:
QS, browser, development, files, UI, whatever.
Brain box:
Linux.
Model runtime.
Weights.
No GUI.
No monitor.
No keyboard.
No audio.
No Windows.
No bullshit.
And your host talks to it.
I'd actually change one thing in your original design:
Don't make USB the main communications path.
Make the little brain a network appliance first, and optionally support USB.
For example:
YOUR MAIN PC
┌───────────────────────┐
│ QS / UI / filesystem  │
│                       │
│ "Load model X"        │
│ "Prompt: hello"       │
└──────────┬────────────┘
│ Ethernet / USB
▼
┌────────────────────────┐
│     LLM BRAIN          │
│                        │
│ Linux                  │
│ model runtime          │
│ weights                │
│ CPU + RAM + accelerator│
│                        │
│  ● boot                │
│  ● loading             │
│  ● thinking            │
│  ● error               │
└────────────────────────┘
The host sends something like:
LOAD MODEL
model.gguf
PROMPT
"Hello, Kelp."
and receives streamed tokens back.
That's almost exactly what QS already does conceptually, except the endpoint is your own hardware instead of somebody else's browser tab.
And your “send the model code & weights from the host” idea is actually feasible—but I wouldn't necessarily resend the weights every time.
I'd make the protocol:
MODEL CHECK
“Do you already have foo.gguf?”
If yes:
“Yep.”
If no:
“Send it.”
Then the brain stores it locally and can reuse it.
That turns your host into the librarian and the little board into the brain.
And THAT part feels particularly Cozmo.
The really interesting possibility
You could make this thing deliberately stupid.
Not stupid inside—the model can be clever.
Stupid outside.
No browser.
No desktop.
No display.
No applications except:
brain_server
and perhaps:
model_runtime
It boots.
Three LEDs:
POWER
READY
That's it.
Your main computer says:
“Kelp, you're needed.”
Brain wakes model.
QS talks to it.
Done.
And suddenly you have your own local Mind endpoint that doesn't care whether Hugging Face is up, whether some company changed its UI, whether a cloud account is suspended, or whether somebody's API changed.
That's a genuinely useful architecture.
The one serious constraint is model size versus memory. Your ancient 4 GB GPU is exactly where you are running into the wall now. The little appliance only becomes exciting once it has enough accelerator-accessible memory to hold the model you actually want. That's why the Jetson/Raspberry Pi examples are interesting but also bounded.
NVIDIA Developer
+1
