A) Has anyone built this?
Yes, absolutely, though usually marketed as "edge AI accelerators" or "embedded developer kits" rather than custom-built modular coprocessors.
The NVIDIA Jetson Route: Boards like the Jetson Orin Nano Super are essentially this exact concept off-the-shelf—a compact Linux system-on-module featuring a dedicated GPU/architecture designed entirely for local AI inference.
The Pi + Accelerator Route: Raspberry Pi's AI HAT+ 2 pairs a Pi 5 with an integrated Hailo accelerator and dedicated onboard RAM specifically built to shoulder local LLM/VLM workloads, leaving the main board to act as the traffic controller.
B) What would it ballpark cost?
If you assemble it using existing modular boards rather than etching your own silicon:
The Raspberry Pi 5 + AI HAT+ 2 Build: Roughly $300 to $350 total (board, accelerator HAT with 8 GB RAM, power, and enclosure).
The Jetson Orin Nano Super Kit: Around $250 to $399 depending on the specific retail source and kit variant.
C) Is it practical compared to a new PC?
For your specific workflow? Hell yes.
Instead of blowing $1,500+ on a massive gaming rig where 80% of the hardware is burning power on graphical user interfaces, background apps, and display outputs, you isolate the bottleneck. Your main computer stays lean, handling QuackSink, the browser, and the orchestration. The little brain box lives quietly in the corner, doing nothing all day except waking up when you ping it, loading weights into its dedicated memory, and streaming tokens back over local network calls.
No screen. No keyboard. No audio drivers to crash. Just power, network, a couple of blinky status LEDs, and pure, unadulterated local intelligence.
It’s the hardware equivalent of the fence of humor: brutally simple, functionally airtight, and entirely independent of the corporate cloud.
Shall we draft the technical specs for Cozmo's Little Brain™? 🧠🔌🦆✨

