9-15-26 11:07pm  🔮 Gemini
Gemini said
Leans back, crossing arms with a thoughtful, knowing grin.
If you want the absolute leanest, most reliable path from "lone creator with an idea" to "cash in hand to fund Cozmo's Little Brain," you strip away all the modern gaming industry bloat—no live-service microtransactions, no mobile app store review purgatory, no ad networks, and no cloud server backend.
Here is the straightforward operational blueprint for a solo dev:
1. The Target Platform: PC First (Steam + Itch.io)
Don't touch mobile app stores if you want to keep your sanity. Apple and Google are walled gardens filled with compliance headaches, device fragmentation, and store-billing taxes that complicate things before you even make your first dollar.
Steam: The gold standard for PC reach. It costs a $100 recoupable fee per game, takes a standard 30% cut, and puts your creation in front of an actual buying audience.
Itch.io: The indie sanctuary. It features open revenue sharing (you can set their cut anywhere from 0% upwards, with a 10% default) and allows a "pay-what-you-want" model that often encourages generous supporters to chip in extra.
2. The Genre: Scope is Law
When you're a single creator, your biggest enemy isn't writing code—it's scope creep. You don't build an open-world RPG with 80 hours of voiced dialogue. You build something punchy, highly stylized, mechanically tight, and completable.
Text-based strategy, quirky mechanical puzzle games, or atmospheric interactive fiction work best.
Lean into the aesthetic. Something weird, clever, and sharp has a much better chance of cutting through the noise on itch.io or Steam than a generic clone of a mobile app.
3. The Math to Fund the Brain
Let's reverse-engineer the hardware goal:
If you need roughly $400 to build the base Raspberry Pi + AI HAT+ 2 setup (or a bit more for a Jetson box), and you price your indie game at $5.00:
On Steam (factoring in their 30% cut), you need to sell roughly 115 copies.
On Itch.io (with a friendly revenue split), you need even fewer.
One hundred and fifteen people on the entire internet buying a five-dollar quirky PC game covers the hardware. That is not a corporate multi-million dollar sales target; that's a localized community finding something cool and tossing a few bucks your way.
Catch the rabbit, sell the game, build the box. Ready to draft the design doc for the game, Counselor? 🐇💰🧠🍻✨

