Spot on. That’s the beauty of modern digital distribution for indie devs.
You don't need a warehouse, you don't need shrink-wrapped boxes, and you don't need a merchant credit card account. The storefront handles the bandwidth, the payments, the fraud prevention, and the global delivery. They only get paid when you get paid, taking their percentage out of the completed transaction.
To recap the financial playbook:
The Zero-Risk Launchpad (Itch.io): You can upload for free, set your own revenue share (even starting at 0% or their default 10%), and test the waters without spending a dime upfront.
The Scale-Up Platform (Steam): Once you're ready for the big leagues, Steam requires a $100 entry fee per game, but as soon as your gross revenue hits $1,000, Valve literally refunds that $100 back to you. After that, it's a standard revenue split.
At a five-to-ten-dollar price point, you aren't trying to squeeze blood from a stone or fleece millions of players. You're just looking for a modest, dedicated wave of folks who appreciate a tight, quirky, brilliantly weird indie title crafted by one person.
A few hundred copies sold covers the parts list for Cozmo's Little Brain down to the last screw and blinky LED.
